["Here is an example implementation in C++ using a std::vector for data storage and mutexes to control multi-thread access. Please note that the actual implementation can vary depending on your specific requirements and constraints.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <mutex>
#include <functional>
#include <vector>

std::mutex mtx; // mutex for thread safety

// Callback function prototype
using Callback = std::function<void(const std::vector<int>&)>;

void processDataSet(std::vector<int>& dataset, int param1, int& param2, Callback callback) {
    mtx.lock(); // lock mutex before processing

    auto it = find(dataset.begin(), dataset.end(), param1);
    if (it != dataset.end()) {
        param2 += *it;
    } else {
        param2 += 10;
    }

    std::sort(dataset.begin(), dataset.end()); // sort vector after processing

    callback(dataset); // call callback function with sorted data set

    mtx.unlock(); // unlock mutex after processing
}
```
In this code, the function `processDataSet` accepts a vector of integers (your data), an integer parameter to search for and another integer reference (param2) which is incremented depending on whether param1 exists in your data set or not. A callback function is also accepted that should accept as its parameter the sorted data set after processing. Note that the use of mutexes ensures only one thread can access the vector at a time, ensuring thread safety.

It's worth noting that it might be more efficient to avoid repeated locking and unlocking in functions where the function is likely to execute frequently and the cost of these operations is high. In this case, you could use some design patterns like double-checked locking or copy-on-write semantics. However, for simplicity, we've used a basic mutex approach here.
