['Here is a basic implementation of `StringHasher` class in Java:

```java
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class StringHasher {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(StringHasher.class);
    private static final String NULL_PLACEHOLDER = "NULL_DATA"; // predefined constant value for missing data

    private static MessageDigest md;
    static {
        try {
            md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }

    // ConcurrentHashMap to store duplicate hash values and their counts
    private static final Map<String, Integer> duplicates = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static String getMD5Hash(String input) {
        if (input == null) return NULL_PLACEHOLDER;

        byte[] hashBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        StringBuilder hexString = new StringBuilder();

        for (byte b : hashBytes) {
            String hex = Integer.toHexString(0xFF & b);
            if (hex.length() == 1) {
                hexString.append('0');
            }
            hexString.append(hex);
        }

        String hash = hexString.toString();
        duplicates.compute(hash, (k, v) -> v == null ? 1 : v + 1); // increment count in map for the duplicate hashes
        if(duplicates.get(hash) > 1){
            logger.info("Duplicate hash value detected: {}", hash);
        }

        return hash;
    }
}
```
This implementation uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to store and count duplicate MD5 hashes. It utilizes the Java standard `MessageDigest` class for generating MD5 hashes, which is thread-safe. The logger tracks duplicate hash values and warns about them in the logs. 

Note that the performance of this implementation will depend on several factors such as your system's CPU capacity, available memory, disk I/O speed etc. You might need to use a profiling tool or performance testing tools to identify any bottlenecks. If it doesn't meet your requirements for throughput and resource usage, consider optimizing or adjusting the implementation based on actual observations and requirements.
']

