['Here is an example implementation using JavaScript. Note that validation of JavaScript syntax and dependencies would require a more complex solution, likely involving AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) parsing libraries or custom implementations. This example assumes correctness in these areas. 

```javascript
function renderFooterScripts(scriptList) {
    let scripts = [];

    scriptList.forEach((scriptObj, index) => {
        const keyRegEx = /^[a-zA-Z_]\w*$/; // Regular Expression for valid identifier

        if (!keyRegEx.test(scriptObj.key)) {
            console.error("Invalid script key at position " + index);
            return;
        }

        if (scriptObj.src && !isValidURL(scriptObj.src)) {
            console.error("Invalid URL for src in script at position " + index);
            return;
        }

        if (scriptObj.code && typeof scriptObj.code !== 'string' || scriptObj.code === '') {
            console.error("Invalid code field in script at position " + index);
            return;
        }

        // Other necessary validations here...

        scripts.push(scriptObj);
    });

    let footerHTML = "";

    scripts.forEach((script) => {
        if (script.src) {
            footerHTML += `<script src="${script.src}"></script>\n`;
        } else if (script.code) {
            // Assumes scriptObj.dependencies contains all scripts that this one depends on, and they have been added to the page already.
            let dependencies = script.dependencies || [];

            if (!dependencies.every(dep => window[dep])) {
                console.error("Unmet dependency for script: " + script.key);
                return;
            }

            footerHTML += `<script>\n${script.code}\n</script>\n`;
        } else {
            console.error("Script object missing src or code field: " + JSON.stringify(script));
        }
    });

    return footerHTML;
}
```
In this implementation, we first validate each script and add it to a list of valid scripts. Then we generate the HTML for the footer by iterating through these scripts. 

We're assuming that all dependencies are already loaded in the `window` object when processing inline scripts. 

Please note, error handling here is quite basic and doesn't actually stop the script from running if there's an error, it just logs to console for debugging purposes. In a production environment, more robust error handling would be required. Also, the URL validation function `isValidURL` is not provided in this example but can be implemented as per your requirement using regular expressions or any library like validator.js.
