['The task you've given is quite a complex one that involves several layers of concurrent programming (multi-threading), network programming, file handling and potentially rate limiting from an external service like AWS S3. Here is a very simplified example to give you the idea:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.AmazonS3;
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.model.GetObjectRequest;
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.model.S3Object;

public class FileTransferManager {
    private static final int BUFFER_SIZE = 8192; // 8KB
    private static final int RETRY_LIMIT = 5;
    private ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());
    private AmazonS3 s3Client;

    public FileTransferManager(AmazonS3 s3Client) {
        this.s3Client = s3Client;
    }

    public void transferFileAsync(String bucketName, String key, AsynchronousFileChannel destination) throws IOException {
        Future<Void> future = executor.submit(() -> {
            int retryCount = 0;
            while (retryCount < RETRY_LIMIT) {
                try (S3Object s3object = s3Client.getObject(new GetObjectRequest(bucketName, key));
                     ReadableByteChannel inChannel = Channels.newChannel(s3object.getObjectContent())) {
                    ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(BUFFER_SIZE);
                    while (inChannel.read(buffer) != -1 || buffer.position() > 0) {
                        buffer.flip(); // Prepare the buffer to be drained
                        destination.write(buffer);
                        buffer.compact(); // Make room for more data to be read into the buffer
                    }
                    return null;
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    retryCount++;
                    System.out.println("An error occurred during file transfer: " + e.getMessage());
                    try {
                        Thread.sleep(1000); // Wait for a second before retrying
                    } catch (InterruptedException ex) {
                        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    }
                }
            }
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to transfer file after " + RETRY_LIMIT + " retries");
        });

        // You could use the future here to provide feedback or wait for completion, etc...
    }
}
```
Please note that above is a simplified example and doesn't take into account things like proper error handling (which should be much more detailed), network interruptions handling, rate limiting constraints from AWS S3, optimal buffer size, different load conditions impacting throughput and latency, horizontal scaling, etc. 

This kind of task would typically require a team of experienced programmers to design, implement, test, and maintain in production environments. The complexity is also noteworthy because it involves the handling of potentially unreliable network connections, file transfers at large scale, integration with third-party services like AWS S3, etc.
']

