['Here is a simplified example using libtiff to process TIFF files and OpenSSL for MD5 hashing. The structure `ImageMetadata` holds both image metadata and MD5 hash of the string. Please note that this solution doesn't include handling unsupported bit depths or exceeding expected sample counts, you may need to add these features yourself.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <openssl/md5.h>
#include <tiffio.h>

typedef struct {
    uint16_t bitDepth;
    uint16_t samplesPerPixel;
    char md5Hash[MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH * 2 + 1]; // hex MD5 hash + null terminator
} ImageMetadata;

ImageMetadata processTIFF(const char* filename, const char* metadata) {
    TIFF* tiff = TIFFOpen(filename, "r");
    if (tiff == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to open TIFF file\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    ImageMetadata metadataResult;
    TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_BITSPERSAMPLE, &metadataResult.bitDepth);
    TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_SAMPLESPERPIXEL, &metadataResult.samplesPerPixel);

    // Compute MD5 hash of metadata string and store it in hex format
    unsigned char md5[MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH];
    MD5((unsigned char*)metadata, strlen(metadata), md5);
    for (int i = 0; i < MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH; ++i) {
        sprintf(&metadataResult.md5Hash[i * 2], "%02x", md5[i]);
    }

    TIFFClose(tiff);
    return metadataResult;
}
```

This function opens a TIFF image file, extracts bit depth and sample composition from its metadata, computes MD5 hash of provided string representation of metadata, and returns this information in the `ImageMetadata` structure. The performance bottleneck here is likely to be disk I/O. To optimize for large images or frequent hashing operations, consider storing the TIFF file on an SSD drive, using a memory-mapped file approach if possible, or utilizing multi-threading. 

Memory usage can be optimized by avoiding unnecessary data copying and minimizing memory allocation inefficiencies. If you need to store extra information for each sample, consider storing them separately instead of including them into the `ImageMetadata` structure to reduce overall size. Always measure performance before and after changes to ensure it's improving what you're optimizing for. 

Bear in mind that this is a simplified solution. Depending on your requirements, you may need to add additional error checking or functionality not covered here. Also keep in mind the potential trade-offs between speed, memory consumption, and ease of maintenance when considering these choices for future scalability.
