['Below is a simple implementation in Java that might meet your requirements. 
Please note that this is just an example, you may want to adjust it based on your specific needs or constraints. The `ClassNotFoundExceptionHandler` is not defined here and would need to be implemented separately.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.Constructor;

public class Deserializer {
    private static final int DEFAULT_MAX_SIZE = 1024 * 1024; // 1MB

    public Object deserialize(byte[] byteArray, Config config) throws ClassNotFoundExceptionHandler, RuntimeException {
        if (!config.isDeserializationEnabled()) {
            try {
                return createPlaceholderInstance(config.getTargetClass());
            } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Could not create placeholder instance", e);
            }
        }

        if (byteArray == null || byteArray.length == 0) {
            System.err.println("Error: Byte array is incomplete");
            return null;
        }

        if(byteArray.length > config.getMaxSizeLimit()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Deserialized data exceeds the predefined maximum size");
        }

        try (ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(byteArray); 
             ObjectInput in = new ObjectInputStream(bis)) {
            return in.readObject();
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new ClassNotFoundExceptionHandler("Deserialized class not found: " + e.getMessage(), e);
        } catch (IOException | RuntimeException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to deserialize object", e);
        }
    }

    private Object createPlaceholderInstance(Class<?> targetClass) throws IllegalStateException {
        try {
            Constructor<?> constructor = targetClass.getConstructor();
            return constructor.newInstance();
        } catch (ReflectiveOperationException e) {
             throw new IllegalStateException("Could not create placeholder instance", e);
        }
    }

    public static class Config {
        private final Class<?> targetClass;
        private boolean deserializationEnabled = true;
        private int maxSizeLimit = DEFAULT_MAX_SIZE;

        public Config(Class<?> targetClass) {
            this.targetClass = targetClass;
        }

        public Class<?> getTargetClass() {
            return targetClass;
        }

        public boolean isDeserializationEnabled() {
            return deserializationEnabled;
        }

        public void setDeserializationEnabled(boolean deserializationEnabled) {
            this.deserializationEnabled = deserializationEnabled;
        }

        public int getMaxSizeLimit() {
            return maxSizeLimit;
        }

        public void setMaxSizeLimit(int maxSizeLimit) {
            if (maxSizeLimit < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid size limit: " + maxSizeLimit);
            this.maxSizeLimit = maxSizeLimit;
        }
    }
}
``` 

This code uses Java's built-in serialization mechanism to deserialize an object from a byte array. It validates the input parameters and provides clear error messages when things go wrong. However, it does not include any logging framework for production use. You could enhance this with a logger like log4j or slf4j based on your specific needs.
