['Below is a simple implementation of the problem using C language with POSIX threads. This code does not cover disk I/O optimization, network buffer sizing or any specific protocol related to how you access remote logs. It also lacks any kind of sophisticated handling for incomplete or malformed entries and it doesn't support concurrent access from multiple clients:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    time_t timestamp;
    int numFields;
} LogEntry;

LogEntry* parseEntry(char* line) {
    LogEntry *entry = malloc(sizeof(LogEntry));
    if (sscanf(line, "%ld %d", &entry->timestamp, &entry->numFields) != 2) {
        free(entry);
        return NULL;
    }
    return entry;
}

void* processFile(void *arg) {
    char* filename = (char*) arg;

    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open %s\n", filename);
        return NULL;
    }

    LogEntry *last_entry = NULL;
    char line[256];
    while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), file)) {
        LogEntry* entry = parseEntry(line);
        if (entry == NULL) continue;  // Skip malformed entries.

        if (last_entry != NULL) {
            double timeDifference =  difftime(entry->timestamp, last_entry->timestamp);
            printf("Time difference: %f\n", timeDifference);
            free(last_entry);
        }

        last_entry = entry;
    }

    fclose(file);

    if (last_entry != NULL) free(last_entry);  // Free the final unprocessed entry.

    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    const char* filename = "log.txt";  // Assuming log is in current directory.

    pthread_t thread1, thread2;
    int result1 = pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, processFile, (void*)filename);
    if (result1 != 0) {
        printf("Failed to create thread 1: %d\n", result1);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    int result2 = pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, processFile, (void*)filename);
    if (result2 != 0) {
        printf("Failed to create thread 2: %d\n", result2);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    pthread_join(thread1, NULL);
    pthread_join(thread2, NULL);

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```
This code assumes that each line in the log file is a timestamp followed by a number. It reads each line and parses it into a LogEntry struct, which it stores for later comparison with the next entry. The time difference between entries is then calculated and printed to standard output. If there are multiple threads reading the same file, they will both process the same data concurrently without synchronization (which would be problematic if each thread needed to access different parts of the file), but you could add locks or other synchronization primitives as necessary for your specific use case. This code also doesn't handle network interruptions and retries; those would require a more complex solution that included appropriate error handling and possibly a lower-level networking library like libcurl.
']

['Here is one way to implement this in C using POSIX system calls like `opendir`, `readdir`, `rename` etc., considering the constraints mentioned. This code might need adjustments based on your actual requirements or if you are working with a specific operating system version which has not been taken into account:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dirent.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <fcntl.h>

void rename_files(const char* dir_path) {
    DIR *dir;
    struct dirent *entry;
    int dirfd = open(dir_path, O_RDONLY | O_DIRECTORY);

    if (dirfd == -1) {
        perror("Could not open directory");
        return;
    }

    if (!(dir = fdopendir(dirfd))) {
        close(dirfd);
        perror("Could not opendir");
        return;
    }

    while ((entry = readdir(dir)) != NULL) {
        // Skip the current and parent directory entries
        if (!strcmp(entry->d_name, ".") || !strcmp(entry->d_name, "..")) continue;

        char old_path[PATH_MAX], new_path[PATH_MAX];
        snprintf(old_path, sizeof(old_path), "%s/%s", dir_path, entry->d_name);

        // Count how many files have the same base name
        int count = 0;
        DIR *tmpdir = opendir(dir_path);
        struct dirent *tmentry;
        while ((tmentry = readdir(tmpdir)) != NULL) {
            if (!strcmp(tmentry->d_name, ".") || !strcmp(tmentry->d_name, "..")) continue;

            char* dot = strrchr(tmentry->d_name, '.');
            int len = (dot) ? dot - tmentry->d_name : strlen(tmentry->d_name);

            if (!strncmp(entry->d_name, tmentry->d_name, len)) count++;
        }

        closedir(tmpdir);

        // Append the unique identifier to the end of the file name
        snprintf(new_path, sizeof(new_path), "%s/%.*s%d", dir_path, len, entry->d_name, count);

        if (rename(old_path, new_path) == -1) {
            perror("Could not rename file");
            continue;
        }
    }

    closedir(dir);
}
```
This is a simple implementation that could benefit from improvements like handling subdirectories, error checking in case of race conditions etc. It also does not handle special file names and potential concurrency issues as mentioned. For example, it might fail if the directory is removed or renamed while we are reading it. To make this function more robust, you would need to add code for error checking and possibly use a more sophisticated method of counting files with similar base names (e.g., using `stat` system call).
']

