['This is a complex task and involves several challenges. Here's an example of how this could be approached in C using pthread library for multithreading, atomic operations for thread safety and bitwise copy function to handle record structures:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
    char* key;
} record;

_Atomic long next_rand = 0;  // atomic counter for unique random values
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;  // mutex for thread safety

void bitwise_copy(record* dest, record* src) {
    memcpy(dest, src, sizeof(record));
}

int generate_unique_random(_Atomic long *next_rand) {
    int val;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);  // acquire lock for atomic operation
    val = ++(*next_rand);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  // release lock
    return val;
}

void* initialize(size_t num_offsets, size_t num_keys, size_t num_records) {
    long *offsets = aligned_alloc(alignof(long), sizeof(long)*num_offsets);
    int *keys = aligned_alloc(alignof(int), sizeof(int)*num_keys);
    record *records = aligned_alloc(alignof(record), sizeof(record)*num_records);

    if (offsets == NULL || keys == NULL || records == NULL) {
        free(offsets);
        free(keys);
        free(records);
        return NULL;  // memory allocation error
    }

    for (size_t i = 0; i < num_offsets; ++i) offsets[i] = 0L;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < num_keys; ++i) keys[i] = generate_unique_random(&next_rand);  // unique random values

    for (size_t i = 0; i < num_records; ++i) {
        records[i].value = generate_unique_random(&next_rand);  // unique random values
        records[i].key = malloc(16);  // allocate memory for key string
        if (records[i].key == NULL) {
            free(offsets);
            free(keys);
            free(records);
            return NULL;  // memory allocation error
        }
        sprintf(records[i].key, "KEY_%d", records[i].value);  // format key string
    }

    return (void*)records;  // return pointer to allocated data
}
```
This implementation uses `aligned_alloc` for aligned memory allocation to optimize cache performance. The `generate_unique_random` function uses a mutex lock and atomic increment operation to ensure unique random values across different instances of the function being called concurrently, while `bitwise_copy` function copies record structures efficiently using bitwise operations.
']

['Below is a basic Java implementation of the task you have defined. Please note that this is a high-level overview and may require further tweaking or extensions based on specific requirements. 

```java
import java.security.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class HashUtils {
    private static final int BACK_PRESSURE_THRESHOLD = 500; // configure based on your system load
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(HashUtils.class);

    public synchronized String getMd5Hash(String input) {
        if (input == null) return "";

        try {
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] hashBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

            for (byte b : hashBytes) 
                sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));

            return sb.toString();
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            // handle error
        }

        return "";
    }

    public void processBatchHashes(List<String> inputStrings) throws InterruptedException {
        Map<String, Integer> hashCounts = new HashMap<>();
        int duplicates = 0;

        for (String str : inputStrings) {
            String hash = getMd5Hash(str);

            if (!hash.isEmpty()) {
                hashCounts.put(hash, hashCounts.getOrDefault(hash, 0) + 1);

                if (hashCounts.get(hash) > 1) { // duplicate detected
                    duplicates++;

                    while (logger.queueSize() > BACK_PRESSURE_THRESHOLD) {
                        Thread.sleep(100); // back-pressure mechanism, adjust as needed
                    }

                    logger.logDuplicateHash(hash);
                }
            }
        }

        int uniqueHashes = hashCounts.size() - duplicates;
        System.out.println("Unique hashes: " + uniqueHashes);
        System.out.println("Duplicates: " + duplicates);
    }
}
```
In the above code, I've added a back-pressure mechanism to prevent logging service from becoming a bottleneck under high load conditions. `BACK_PRESSURE_THRESHOLD` is used for configuring this mechanism. This can be adjusted based on your system load. If queue size exceeds threshold, the thread sleeps for 100ms before trying again to add to the log.

Please note that you need to replace the Logger instance with an actual logger implementation and `logger.queueSize()` method with the correct method call to get current queue size of your logging service.

Also, please keep in mind this is a simplistic approach and doesn't include all the configurations mentioned for handling large volumes of strings, batch processing framework, integration into larger distributed systems, error handling etc. It can serve as an initial starting point based on your requirements but you might need to add more complexity or adjustments based on specific scenarios.
']

