```javascript
const { Transform } = require('stream');

class DynamicStreamAdapter extends Transform {
    constructor(options) {
        super(options);
        this._bufferSize = options.bufferSize || 1024; // buffer size in bytes
        this._interval = options.interval || 500;      // processing interval in ms
        this._dataRate = 0;                           // current data rate in bytes/ms
    }

    _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
        let now = Date.now();

        // Calculate and update the data rate
        this._dataRate += chunk.length / (this._interval / 1000);

        if (this._dataRate > this._bufferSize) {
            console.error('Buffer overflow, processing interval too short');
            callback(new Error('Buffer overflow'));
            return;
        }

        // Do some transformation on the chunk...

        setTimeout(() => {
            // Decrease data rate after the interval has passed
            this._dataRate -= chunk.length / (this._interval / 1000);
            callback(null, transformedChunk);
        }, this._interval);
    }
}
```
This is a simple implementation of a Transform stream that dynamically adapts to varying data rates and network conditions by checking the size of incoming chunks against an internal buffer. It uses `setTimeout` to delay processing the chunk, which allows it to control memory usage and performance. 

Please note that this code doesn't cover many of the features you mentioned in your request, such as seamless integration with existing systems or external data sources, error handling, logging mechanisms for maintaining high reliability, and flexible scalability requirements. These would require a more complex solution that is beyond the scope of this example. You may want to use an established streaming library like Apache Kafka or frameworks like Node.js's `stream` API in combination with libraries like `async` and `axios` for external data sources.
