4. Implement a function to fetch multiple pokemons in parallel:
```rust
pub struct Client {
    base_url: Url,
    http_client: reqwest::Client,
    runtime: Arc<Runtime>,
}
impl Client {
    pub fn new(base_url: &str) -> Result<Self, hyper::http::Error> {
        let http_client = reqwest::ClientBuilder::new().build()?;
        Ok(Self {
            base_url: base_url.parse()?,
            http_client,
            runtime: Arc::new(tokio::runtime::Runtime::new()?),
        })
    }
    pub async fn get_pokemons(&self, names: &[String]) -> Result<Vec<Pokemon>, reqwest::Error> {
        let mut handles = Vec::with_capacity(names.len());
        for name in names {
            let http_client = self.http_client.clone();
            handles.push(self.runtime.spawn(async move {
                let res = http_client.get(format::fmt(&self.base_url, "pokemon/{}", name).as_ref()).send().await?;
                Ok(res.json().await?)
            }));
        }
        futures::future::join_all(handles).await
    }
}
```
Please note that handling rate limits, caching and mapping pokemon types to their weaknesses are not included in this example code and you would need to implement these yourself. You might want to use an external crate for caching such as `cachemap` or write your own implementation. The same goes with the mapping from Pokemon type to its weaknesses which can be implemented using a hashmap where types map to their weaknesses.
