```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class Aggregator {
    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
    private volatile int uniqueCount = 0;
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, Integer> duplicateDetection = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void aggregateDataAsync(InputStream inputStream, int initialValue, BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> callback) {
        try (Scanner scanner = new Scanner(inputStream)) {
            while (scanner.hasNext()) {
                final int value = scanner.nextInt();
                CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> callback.apply(initialValue, value), executor)
                        .thenApply(result -> {
                            duplicateDetection.compute(result, (k, v) -> v == null ? 1 : v + 1);
                            return result;
                        })
                        .whenComplete((r, t) -> {
                            if (t != null) {
                                // handle error
                            } else {
                                uniqueCount++;
                            }
                        });
            }
        }
    }

    public int getUniqueCount() {
        return uniqueCount;
    }

    public Map<Integer, Integer> getDuplicateDetection() {
        return duplicateDetection;
    }
}
```
This code creates an asynchronous task for each input value. It uses a `CompletableFuture` to perform the aggregation operation and process the result in another thread. The `whenComplete` method is used to update some statistics when the future completes, regardless of whether it succeeded or failed. This implementation does not handle errors, back pressure, configuration parameters or any performance optimizations such as reducing object allocations or minimizing integration impact with an external service. Also note that this code assumes all input values are integers and won't compile if other types are used.
