['This task involves multiple aspects of programming, such as file I/O, memory management, error handling, concurrency control, logging, and optimization. Here is a simple example how you might approach it in C using POSIX threads (pthreads) for synchronization. This code doesn't account for versioning or timeout yet but should give an idea of what kind of functions to write:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct bundle {
    char **items;
    int count;
} Bundle;

pthread_mutex_t loadMutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
FILE *logFile;

Bundle* loadBundle(const char *path) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&loadMutex);

    FILE *file = fopen(path, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        fprintf(logFile, "Failed to open file %s\n", path);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&loadMutex);
        return NULL;
    }

    Bundle *bundle = malloc(sizeof(Bundle));
    if (bundle == NULL) {
        fprintf(logFile, "Failed to allocate memory for bundle\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&loadMutex);
        return NULL;
    }

    char buffer[256];
    int size = 0;
    while (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), file)) {
        if (size % 10 == 0) { // resize array every 10 items
            bundle->items = realloc(bundle->items, (size + 10) * sizeof(char*));
            if (bundle->items == NULL) {
                fprintf(logFile, "Failed to allocate memory for bundle items\n");
                free(bundle);
                pthread_mutex_unlock(&loadMutex);
                return NULL;
            }
        }

        size_t len = strlen(buffer);
        char *item = malloc((len + 1) * sizeof(char)); // +1 for null terminator
        if (item == NULL) {
            fprintf(logFile, "Failed to allocate memory for item\n");
            free(bundle->items);
            free(bundle);
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&loadMutex);
            return NULL;
        }

        strcpy(item, buffer); // copy string including null terminator
        bundle->items[size++] = item;
    }

    fclose(file);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&loadMutex);
    return bundle;
}
```
This code uses `fgets` to read lines from the file, which should be enough for simple text files. Each line is stored as a dynamically allocated C-string in an array of pointers to strings (items). The size of this array automatically resizes as needed to accommodate new items. 

Note: This code doesn't handle errors correctly. In real code, you should use `errno` for error messages and check the return values from all library functions that can fail. You should also release any allocated resources if an error occurs before exiting the function. 

Error handling is simplified in this example. In a real program, you would likely need more complex logic to correctly handle errors and manage resource cleanup. Additionally, timeout handling and versioning require additional code.
']

