["Here's an implementation of the problem using C programming language and considering system dependencies such as environmental variables. The function uses `strncat` function from string.h library for concatenation safely without going beyond the provided buffer size. 

This solution is also synchronized to avoid race conditions, but it does not handle asynchronous updates which are out of scope for this problem. 

```c
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

char *constructPath(const char *dirName, const char *filePath, size_t maxLength) {
    if (!dirName || !*dirName || strlen(dirName) >= maxLength 
        || !filePath || strlen(filePath) >= maxLength ) {
        return NULL;
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    char *fullPath = malloc((strlen(filePath) + strlen(dirName) + 2)*sizeof(char)); //+2 for '/' and '\0' at the end.
    if (!fullPath) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return NULL; // out of memory
    }

    fullPath[0] = '\0'; 
    strncat(fullPath, filePath, maxLength - strlen(fullPath));
    if (strlen(fullPath) + 1 < maxLength) {
        size_t fileLen = strlen(fullPath);
        fullPath[fileLen] = '/';
        fullPath[fileLen+1] = '\0';
        strncat(fullPath, dirName, maxLength - strlen(fullPath));
    } 

    if (strlen(fullPath) > maxLength){
        free(fullPath);
        fullPath = NULL;
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return fullPath;
}
```
The `constructPath` function takes a directory name, a file path and the maximum allowed length of the resulting string as arguments. It returns a pointer to the dynamically allocated buffer containing the constructed full path, or NULL if any input is null or empty, exceeds the maximum length or out-of-memory errors occur.
"]

