["The problem is complex, thus, providing a fully working solution in C can be challenging due to several factors like error handling, memory management, concurrency, scalability, etc. However, I'll provide the base structure that you can expand upon. For brevity and focusing on key parts only, I've omitted some of the details like error handling, thread-safety measures, memory optimization or advanced data structures used for balancing and grouping entries.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct entry_t {
    int id;
    char* str;
    size_t refcount;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;  // used for synchronization in concurrent access
    struct entry_t *next;  // linked list pointer
} entry_t;

// Comparator function to sort entries
int compare(const void *a, const void *b) {
    return ((entry_t*)a)->id - ((entry_t*)b)->id;
}

typedef struct group_t {
    int rangeStart;
    int rangeEnd;
    size_t count;  // number of entries in this group
    entry_t *entries;  // linked list of entries in this group
    pthread_mutex_t lock;  // used for synchronization when rebalancing the groups
} group_t;

// Function to insert an entry into a specific group
void insertEntry(group_t* group, entry_t* newEntry) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&group->lock);

    if (group->entries == NULL) {
        // first entry in the group
        group->entries = newEntry;
    } else {
        entry_t *current = group->entries;

        while(current->next != NULL && compare(&(current->next), &newEntry) < 0) {
            current = current->next;
        }

        newEntry->next = current->next;
        current->next = newEntry;
    }

    group->count++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&group->lock);
}
```
Please note that this is a very simplified version of what you're asking for. It only shows how to handle the insertion and comparison of entries in a linked list, and it uses mutexes (pthread_mutex_t) for synchronization which may not be ideal for many cases as they are heavyweight and can introduce contention during high loads.

For memory efficiency, you could use a custom allocator to minimize fragmentation. For scalability, you might want to consider using hash tables or other data structures that scale better than linked lists. Concurrency can be addressed in various ways depending on the exact requirements of your application (e.g., locks, fine-grained locks, lock-free data structures). Error handling is also a big aspect to cover and often requires specific domain knowledge.
