["Here is a simple example of how you might structure this function in JavaScript:

```javascript
class DOMQuery {
  constructor() {
    this.cache = {}; // caching mechanism
  }

  getElements(selectors, options) {
    const elements = [];

    for (let selector of selectors) {
      if (!this.cache[selector]) {
        this.cache[selector] = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(selector));
      }

      // filter based on options
      if (options && options.attributes) {
        for (let attr of options.attributes) {
          this.cache[selector] = this.cache[selector].filter(el => el.getAttribute(attr));
        }
      }

      elements.push(...this.cache[selector]);
    }

    return elements;
  }
}
```
This class can be integrated into an existing framework by creating a single instance of `DOMQuery` and calling `getElements` as needed. The cache is used to avoid querying the DOM for selectors that have already been queried, which minimizes reprocessing of the entire tree during real-time changes.

