['Here is a basic Java code block implementing all your specifications:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class FileProcessor {
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public List<String> processFiles(List<Path> filePaths) {
        return filePaths.parallelStream()
                .map(this::readFileContent)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    private String readFileContent(Path path) {
        try {
            if (!cache.containsKey(path)) {
                byte[] bytes = Files.readAllBytes(path);
                cache.putIfAbsent(path, new String(bytes));
            } 
            return cache.getOrDefault(path, "");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Failed to read file: " + path.toString());
            return "";
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we use a `ConcurrentHashMap` as an in-memory cache for file contents, which allows us to avoid redundant reads and improve performance when processing large directories with thousands of files. However, please note that the tradeoff here is memory usage - each unique file will consume space in the map regardless of its size or content. This approach might not be optimal for very large files where reading them all into memory may not be feasible.

In this example, we use parallel stream to process files concurrently, but it does not handle potential race conditions between threads as per your requirement, since `ConcurrentHashMap` ensures thread safety by default in its methods. 

Finally, we use a try-catch block to manage I/OExceptions that might occur while reading the file content, and return an empty string in such cases. This way, our method will not fail when encountering unexpected IO operations but instead handle them gracefully.
