```
The `Node` structure acts as a single element in the linked list. Each node contains a mutex which is locked when accessing its data to prevent concurrent modification from multiple threads, and also freeing that memory will require acquiring the lock.

Here's some pseudo-code for a simple thread-safe linked list:
```c
Node* head = NULL;  // Global variable

// Add a node to the end of the list (after locking it)
void add_node(SessionState *data){
    Node *new_node = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    new_node->next = NULL;
    new_node->data = data;  // deep copy your actual SessionState here

    pthread_mutex_init(&new_node->lock, NULL);

    if (head == NULL){
        head = new_node;
    } else {
        Node *current = head;
        while(current->next != NULL){
            current = current->next;
        }
        current->next = new_node;
    }
}

// Remove a node from the list (after locking it)
void remove_node(Node *node){
    if (head == node){
        head = node->next;
    } else {
        Node *current = head;
        while(current != NULL && current->next != node){
            current = current->next;
        }
        if (current != NULL){
            current->next = node->next;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_destroy(&node->lock);
    free(node->data);  // deep free your actual SessionState here
    free(node);
}
```
Remember, this is a high level pseudo code and not complete code, it's only to give you an idea how linked list can be made thread safe. The code will have many more features including memory management, session expiration mechanism, parameter validation, logging system etc. Also please note, designing such a module would require deep understanding of C language features, concurrent programming and systems design.
