['Due to the complexity of this task, it is not possible to provide a complete code module in one response. However, I can guide you through some basic steps that may assist you in creating your caching module for managing themes in a mobile application. 

Firstly, you will need to create an `AssetManager` class that manages theme bitmap recycling, default theme directory configuration, and concurrent access with thread-safe mechanisms to maintain data integrity. This can be done using the following code:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class AssetManager {
    private static final int MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 100; // define your own max cache size
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, Bitmap> bitmapCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private LinkedList<String> lruQueue = new LinkedList<>();

    public void addBitmap(String key, Bitmap bitmap) {
        if (bitmapCache.size() >= MAX_CACHE_SIZE) {
            String oldestKey = lruQueue.removeFirst();
            bitmapCache.remove(oldestKey);
        }
        lruQueue.addLast(key);
        bitmapCache.put(key, bitmap);
    }

    public Bitmap getBitmap(String key) {
        lruQueue.remove(key);
        lruQueue.addLast(key); // move to end of queue
        return bitmapCache.get(key);
    }
}
```
This class uses a combination of ConcurrentHashMap for thread safety and an LRU Queue to manage the cache size. When adding a new Bitmap, it checks if the cache is full and removes the oldest item from both the queue and the map before adding the new item. 

