['This problem requires deep understanding of C programming, memory management, multi-process synchronization, data structures, and concurrency issues. The following is an attempt at providing a starting point for this kind of function but note that it's quite complex and may require further adjustments to suit your specific requirements or constraints. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct Setting {
    char* name;
    int value;
    struct Setting* ref_setting;
} Setting;

Setting* settings = NULL; // Global linked list of settings
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void insert(char* name, int value, Setting** ref_setting) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Insert new setting at the beginning of list
    Setting* new_node = malloc(sizeof(Setting));
    new_node->name = strdup(name);
    new_node->value = value;
    if (ref_setting) {
        new_node->ref_setting = *ref_setting; 
    } else {
        new_node->ref_setting = NULL;
    }

    // Check for memory allocation errors
    if (!new_node || !(new_node->name)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation error!\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    new_node->next = settings;
    settings = new_node;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

void delete(char* name) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    Setting** current = &settings;

    while (*current != NULL) {
        if (strcmp((*current)->name, name) == 0) {
            Setting* temp = *current;
            // Update the linked list
            *current = (*current)->next;

            free(temp->name);
            free(temp);

            break;
        }

        current = &((*current)->next);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

void modify(char* name, int value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    Setting* node = settings;

    while (node != NULL) {
        if (strcmp(node->name, name) == 0) {
            node->value = value; 
            break;
        }

        node = node->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

int query(char* name) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    Setting* node = settings;

    while (node != NULL) {
        if (strcmp(node->name, name) == 0) {
            int value = node->value; 

            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            return value;
        }

        node = node->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return -1; // Return a special error value if setting not found
}
```

This is an elementary example and it doesn't take into consideration many aspects you mentioned, such as dynamic memory management in terms of memory usage per process or node. Further optimization could be done by using shared memory for sharing settings between processes or nodes instead of passing them through the network, for instance. 

Also, concurrency control mechanisms are implemented only with a simple mutex lock and it's assumed that all functions are thread-safe. However, in a real-world distributed system you would likely need to use more robust synchronization primitives like semaphores or condition variables. 

Remember to call `pthread_mutex_destroy(&mutex);` before program exit and also free any dynamically allocated memory when it is no longer needed to prevent memory leaks. 

This code does not handle all possible errors, you should check the return value of malloc() or strdup() and adjust your error handling accordingly. And please note that C doesn't have garbage collection like languages such as Python or Java do, so you must manually manage memory.
']

